@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
title Backpropagation — Learning Representations by Propagating Errors
' Paper: Learning representations by back-propagating errors
' URL:   https://doi.org/10.1038/323533a0
' Level: 1

component "Input layer\nx" as IN <<app>>
component "Hidden layers\nnon-linear units" as HID <<app>>
component "Output layer\nprediction ŷ" as OUT <<app>>
component "Loss\ncompare ŷ to target y" as LOSS <<error>>
component "Backward pass\n∂Loss/∂w by chain rule" as BACK <<async>>
database  "Weights w\nshared state" as W <<db>>

IN ARROW_MAIN HID : forward pass
HID ARROW_MAIN OUT
OUT ARROW_MAIN LOSS : compare to target
LOSS ARROW_MAIN BACK : error signal,\npropagated backwards
BACK ARROW_QUEUE W : gradient per weight
W ARROW_OPTIONAL HID : updated parameters\nfeed the next forward pass

note bottom of BACK
  The contribution is **cheap credit assignment**:
  one backward sweep yields ∂Loss/∂w for //every//
  weight at roughly the cost of one forward pass,
  by reusing each layer's intermediate derivative.
  That is why hidden layers became trainable — and
  why "hidden units learn useful internal
  representations" is the paper's actual claim.
  ----
  Backprop produces the **gradient only**. Turning
  a gradient into a step is a separate choice:
  ""../optimization/gradient-descent.puml"".
end note
@enduml
